Wachturm

A Docker-ready API monitoring system built with .NET 8, Clean Architecture, PostgreSQL, and a background worker.

Wachturm is a backend monitoring service for registering HTTP endpoints, checking them on independent schedules, storing check history, and exposing clean API endpoints for health, latest result, endpoint status, history, and dashboard summary. The system is split into `wachturm.Api`, `wachturm.Worker`, `wachturm.Application`, `wachturm.Domain`, and `wachturm.Infrastructure`, keeping controllers thin while validation, DTO mapping, use-case handlers, repository abstractions, and status/statistics calculations live in the Application layer. It supports endpoint CRUD, activation/deactivation, common HTTP method validation, timeout threshold configuration, latency warnings, basic alert detection after three consecutive failures, Scalar API documentation at `/scalar/v1`, and a simple `/health` endpoint.

The project is designed as a polished portfolio/CV backend project that can be run locally with `docker compose up --build`, starting PostgreSQL, the API on `http://localhost:8080`, and the worker service together. It uses EF Core with `EnsureCreated` instead of migrations for now, Serilog console structured logging for both API and Worker, environment-based Docker connection strings, and focused xUnit tests for endpoint creation validation and status calculation. Verification commands are `dotnet build` and `dotnet test`; intentionally excluded for this version are authentication, frontend UI, RabbitMQ, Redis, and EF Core migrations.
